+The concurrency mechanism belongs to record-store, not this HLD. Tablet-sync and review-export size their designs against the record-store contract: it determines the submission envelope, the shape of review work, and which record states enter an export. See the record-store design for those definitions.
+
+Specific obligations each node inherits from the whole-visit revisions choice:
+
+- **tablet-sync** must carry a base revision ID with every edit submission (identifying the revision the worker last fetched) and must surface the `under_review` visit status returned by the server without treating it as a submission error.
+- **review-export** must provide a coordinator view listing visits with competing revisions, support side-by-side comparison of those revisions, and exclude any visit with unresolved competing revisions from the export set.
+
+## Key Design Decisions
+
+### Whole-visit revisions for concurrent edits
+
+When two workers independently edit the same visit from the same base revision, both submissions are stored as complete competing revisions. The visit is held out of export until a coordinator accepts one. The alternative is field-aware three-way merge: the server automatically combines edits to disjoint fields (e.g., one worker corrects an observer name, another adds a missing species) and routes only overlapping fields to coordinator review, reducing the volume of visits that need attention.
+
+Field-aware merge was set aside because it requires maintained rules for combining scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Those rules could produce scientifically plausible but incorrect combinations when workers intended competing interpretations — for example, different species identifications paired with different cover estimates. A merged record would look resolved while reflecting neither worker's intent, which works against the *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* tenet.
+
+A larger review queue is the trade-off. With 6,000 visits per season and 24 workers, if coordinators find themselves routinely approving edits to clearly unrelated fields, review volume could outpace two people before Friday. Field-aware merge is the natural response to that pressure; revisit this choice then with observed queue data. Details and mechanics are in the record-store design.
 
